+## Mentions
+
+Mention links are **side-effecting actions**:
+
+- `[MUL-123](mention://issue/<issue-id>)` — clickable link (no side effect)
+- `[Project Name](mention://project/<project-id>)` — clickable link (no side effect)
+- `[@Name](mention://member/<user-id>)` — **notifies a human**
+- `[@Name](mention://agent-notify/<agent-id>)` — wakes the agent without creating a new result return. Use for notifications. If the same agent is also delegated to, delegation wins; a squad mention delegates to its leader even if the leader is also notified.
+- `[@Name](mention://agent/<agent-id>)` — delegates and **enqueues a new run for that agent**; an external agent bound to a host session gets one mailbox request instead, delivered by its host adapter
+
+A mention pulls someone into work they are not doing yet: escalate to a human owner, hand another agent a concrete new sub-task, loop someone in because the user asked. It is not needed merely to notify — followers of the issue already see your comment in the GUI, and completion notifications are platform-owned, but routine sub-issue activity may create no human Inbox notification; mention a human only when their decision, approval, intervention, or explicitly requested notification is needed. Routine child progress, delivery, and internal coordinator review are not reasons to mention the human. Nor is it how a name is written — crediting a decision or citing someone's earlier point is prose about them, not work for them; the link form dispatches whoever it names, so a reference stays plain text. A thank-you / sign-off / FYI mention of another agent enqueues a paid run whose only possible reply is another courtesy; a missed mention costs one follow-up ask, a stray one costs a run. Silence ends conversations.
+
+Only an explicit @ is a handoff. An ordinary comment, a reply, or a read receipt starts no run, and a status change is display state rather than a request. When your result needs the leader, coordinator, or next executor to act, @-mention that agent in the result comment. Address a next-action handoff to the registered recipient: a managed agent gets a dispatched run, an external session gets one mailbox request. A coordinator with no registered identity is not woken, and a child's `done` wakes its parent's assignee by stage — reach any other coordinator with an explicit mention.
